<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spessart</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spessart is a range of low, densely wooded hills in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, enclosed on three sides by the River Main. For centuries it was a hunting forest of the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, and the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg">Aschaffenburg</a> formed part of their territory. Its oak and beech woods are among the largest continuous forests in Germany.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spessart.jpg" alt="Spessart" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Drow69 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rohrberg_Nebel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Spessart is a Mittelgebirge, a range of low wooded mountains in the German states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vogelsberg">Vogelsberg</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhon">Rhön</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odenwald">Odenwald</a>. Its extent can also be described by its rivers: the Main to the south and west, the Kinzig to the north and the Sinn to the north-east. It covers about 2,440 square kilometres, of which 1,710 lie in Bavaria, and its highest point is the Geiersberg at 586 metres.</p><p>The name derives from Spechtshardt, from the German Specht, woodpecker, and Hardt, an old word for hilly forest. Its lowest strata are gneiss and mica schist about 1,200 million years old, and most of the Hochspessart is capped by red sandstone up to 400 metres thick, the stone used for many public buildings including Mainz Cathedral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People have lived in the region since at least the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a> attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-tene-culture">La Tène culture</a> were built there between 500 and 100 BCE. The Roman frontier, the Limes, met the Main at Großkrotzenburg and followed the river south to Miltenberg, so the Spessart proper lay outside the empire, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> kept the northern Vorspessart as an uninhabited buffer zone. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> came in the late 3rd century and the Allemanni ousted them in the 5th, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> absorbed the Allemanni.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> the forests were used for hunting, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s reign they were a royal hunting preserve. The uplands were settled only after abbeys were founded, among them the Benedictine abbey of Neustadt am Main around 770, which Charlemagne endowed with woodland.</p><p>In 974 Emperor Otto II gave much of the former royal preserve to the collegiate church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg">Aschaffenburg</a>, and in 982 Otto I, Duke of Swabia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, left his territories in the area to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>. Between the 12th and 15th centuries the Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">Electors</a> and Archbishops of Mainz and other nobles built hunting lodges and moated castles such as Mespelbrunn, and in the 14th century Mainz set up local officers to supervise hunting, forestry, fishing and the rafting of logs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>About 85 percent of the Hochspessart is covered by forest, mainly oak and beech. The Bavarian Spessart Nature Park, set up in 1961 as the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, covers 171,000 hectares and has the largest continuous mixed forest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>; the Hessian park followed in 1962 and covers 72,900 hectares. No cities or large towns lie inside the parks. The Nordspessart has more evergreens because its original tree cover was largely gone by the 15th century, owing to the fuel needs of local glass foundries. Beavers, hunted to extinction in the region, have been re-established since the 1980s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spessart” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They held lands in the <b>Spessart</b> and the Main valley as vassals of the bishops of Würzburg and gradually built a small territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-wertheim">County of Wertheim</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Electorate of Mainz lost its left-bank lands, the Imperial Recess of 1803 formed its remaining territories on the Main and <b>Spessart</b> into the Principality of Aschaffenburg for Karl Theodor von Dalberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-aschaffenburg">Principality of Aschaffenburg</a></li></ul></section>
